welcome everybody and thanks for  attending today we're going to talk  about some tools that you could use to  write larger shiny apps that are easy to  think about or to reuse shiny code  across apps and these tools are called  modules so let's start with the question  you what is a shiny module and  conceptually it's pretty easy to answer  a tiny module is a self-contained  composable component of a shiny app  self-contained means it's sort of like  an R function it does something and it  does it all by itself it might take some  input it might return some output but in  the middle of the process the app the  module doesn't need help from anything  else to come to do its job and because  of this shiny modules are composable you  could take multiple modules and pull  them together to make a shiny app and  probably the easiest way to understand  really where shiny app does and what it  looks like is to look at actual shiny  app that uses modules so let's go to our  studio what I have here is a single file  shiny app it makes this app over here  which is basically a recreation of hands  Rosling's Gapminder animations so what I  have is you know some different metrics  about all different countries on the  planet and particularly the life  expectancy those countries versus their  GDP and I have a slider down here that I  could play and it will show how things  have changed over time for these  different countries which which creates  a compelling story in its own way but  mostly I want to look at the app as it  is this app has a graph and a slider and  the two are connected in a way that you  could play the graph across time using  the slider in the app also several  different tabs if we look at any of the  tabs there just sub views of this data  so we get to look at countries on a  continent by continent basis except for  year final notes got on there we could  for any of these cots  we could play the animation and just see  you know how things change for African  countries for example or American  countries and if you think about the way  the app is designed there's a lot of  repetition here each tab each view does  the same thing it shows a graph and the  graph is connected to a slider the one  thing that's different between the  different views is the actual data set  that appears in the graph and if we come  over here and look at the code for the  app we see that you know pass because of  this there's a lot of repetition in the  code we do the same thing for each of  the different tabs on the server side we  have to recreate some more processes to  make the graph and connect it to the  slider there's reactive values in there  and it ends up being a fairly long app  and it's a little hard to find any  particular piece of code because there's  so much code to wade through but not  only that the code looks really similar  to other parts of the coding it's kind  of hard to be certain about where you're  at yeah now I've taken the time to  rewrite this app using shiny modules and  when you use modules the app looks like  this as much more concise it's much  smaller and what I've done is I've done  is I've written an R script that takes  the common pieces of code for each of  the tabs in our app and saves them the  functions so here in create a function  called gap module you I know a function  called gap module gap module does all  the server-side stuff to make that graph  connect to the slider and then in my  modulized  app i load that our script and instead  of calling you know visualize slider  here's what I want to graph here's what  I want them to connect I just call the  functions that do that for me so I'm  calling gap module six times once for  each view of the data and then I'm  calling you know the server side of gap  module six times once for each view of  the data this app is much easier to  understand as much easier to look at and  if I want to get in saying these  individual pieces and I could look at  the definition of gap module UI or gap  module over here so this is the  difference between a module wise DAP and  an app that's not much motorized once we  took the time to write our modules we  can recall them as many times as we want  so I've called gap module six times here  but I can also call it in a different  app so this is a great way to write some  code that you can reuse later on you  find for example you're always creating  some sort of upload API  in your apps you can create that API  once and then call that module across  all your apps and reuse it so that's  what modules are like conceptually  here's the here's our module in that  last app why would you use these modules  well you could see that it's very easy  to reuse this code modules are sorta  like functions once you take the time to  write them you could call them whenever  you like to redo whatever that function  does in this case what it does is create  components of Chania the next thing  about modules are that they really  isolate the functionality in your apps  once you write your module function all  you have to think about is what that  function does when you see it in your  app you don't have to think about how it  does what it does
 

